{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The incident\n\n2026-09-04, ~21:28-22:09 UTC (41 minutes). The Gerrit host stopped serving entirely: HTTP 000\nconnection timeouts on every endpoint, all `rebar/host` metric publishing ceased. Ten alarms fired\ncorrectly. Recovered by an operator-authorised stop/start.\n\n## CONFIRMED ROOT CAUSE (Phase 1 complete — full evidence in the comments)\n\nTwo mechanisms, one incident.\n\n**H2 (sustainer, experimentally confirmed).** `infra/scripts/observability.sh` re-scans the ENTIRE\nretained journal on every run with no `--since`, no `--lines` and no `timeout`: four fixed scans\n(`:760`, `:788`, `:816`, `:976`) plus one inside `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta` (`:883`) that\nruns once per marker call site — 13 whole-journal scans per run on `origin/main` today.\n`infra/terraform/user_data.sh` sets no journald tuning, so `SystemMaxUse` defaults to 4 GiB.\nMeasured: the 300 s timer period is exhausted at ~1.37 GB of retained journal; 1.86 GB took 407.5 s\n(136% of the period); 4 GiB projects to ~940 s and ~45.6 GB read. Control: same journal, unbounded\n33,936 ms vs `-n 5000` 798 ms — 42.5x. The journald field index does not reduce bytes read.\n\n`infra/scripts/install-observability.sh:20-27` declares `Type=oneshot` with no `TimeoutStartSec`\n(systemd reports `TimeoutStartUSec=infinity`), and `:31` sets `OnUnitActiveSec=5min`, computed from\nthe last COMPLETED activation. Confirmed on real systemd 255: a hung run does not delay the timer,\nit DELETES its next elapse (`NextElapseUSecMonotonic=infinity`). One overrun latches the probe off\npermanently.\n\n**H1 (trigger, evidenced).** Not memory exhaustion — total anon RSS was ~4.16 GB of 8 GiB. A page\ncache eviction at ~14:26 turned scans that had been served from cache (~28 IOPS in the 14:05-14:25\nlull) into cold reads. From then the run overran, root EBS pinned at ~2,580 IOPS (86% of the gp3\n3,000 provisioned), and nothing could re-warm the cache.\n\n## Defect attribution\n\nTwo origins, recorded in full in the attribution comment:\n\n- **`786b-190d-5e3a-4194`** (`smelly-pane-bolt`), commit `6cd6f4e1f7c0` — created the unit with\n  `Type=oneshot`, no `TimeoutStartSec`, and `OnUnitActiveSec=5min` in one hunk. The latch.\n- **`34cd-1a7c-b9b1-4b05`** (`equestrian-clueless-cowbird`), commit `e9d4d390c364` — put a complete\n  unbounded journal scan inside a reusable PER-MARKER helper, making scan cost linear in the number\n  of published markers. The overrun.\n\n## Why this is a defect, not a tuning miss\n\n`observability.sh:288-292` states the rule and bounds its own docker calls by it: *\"a wedged daemon\nunder disk pressure must not hold the 5-minute timer open\"*. The 13 journal scans violate that rule\nin the same file, and the unit violates it again by omitting `TimeoutStartSec`. Separately, the\nprobe's stated contract is that *\"a probe that could not measure publishes NOTHING rather than a\nplausible 0\"* and that *\"its ABSENCE means the probe/timer/host is dead\"* (`:280`, `:487`). A probe\nthat can silence itself by hanging inverts its own heartbeat semantics: absence came to mean \"the\nprobe is stuck\" while reading identically to \"the host is dead\".\n\n## REPAIR PLAN\n\n### R1 — bound the journal scans by replacing the count-offset design, not by bolting on a timeout\n\nThe delta design stores a cumulative COUNT and derives the interval delta as `total - prev`. That\nis what makes re-scanning everything structural: a cumulative total can only be recomputed from the\nstart of the journal. Two wrong repairs are therefore rejected up front:\n\n- Adding `--since` while keeping the arithmetic turns `total` into a WINDOW count, so `total - prev`\n  becomes a garbage delta.\n- Adding only a `timeout` produces a SILENTLY TRUNCATED count, and `total - prev` then publishes a\n  plausible wrong number. A wrong number is worse than a missing one, and the script's own contract\n  at `:280`/`:487` says a probe that cannot measure publishes nothing.\n\nSo the design changes. Each counter persists a **timestamp cursor** instead of a cumulative count;\nthe scan counts matches strictly after that cursor with `--since`, and that count IS the delta — no\nsubtraction. Per-run journal reads become one interval (~5 minutes) instead of the whole retention.\n\nConsequences that are part of the design, not side effects:\n- Cursors are persisted to NEW files, so an existing count offset file is inert rather than\n  misinterpreted as an epoch (both are bare integers).\n- The scheme is immune to rotation and `--vacuum`, which removes the `new < 0` fudge and the whole\n  bug class of \"counters recount on rotation\".\n- First run seeds the cursor to now and publishes 0, preserving the existing seed behaviour.\n- The scan is additionally wall-clock bounded. On timeout or read failure the cursor is NOT\n  advanced and NOTHING is published for that metric; the next successful run covers the gap because\n  the cursor still points at the last instant actually counted.\n\n### R2 — give the unit a finite start timeout and deprioritise its I/O\n\n`install-observability.sh` renders `TimeoutStartSec` strictly less than the timer period, so a hung\nrun is killed before the next elapse and can never overlap it. With a finite start timeout the\nactivation always completes, so `OnUnitActiveSec=5min` re-arms on its own — the latch is removed at\nits root rather than papered over with a second trigger.\n\nThe unit also gains `Nice=10` and `IOSchedulingClass=idle`, matching `rebar-autodeploy.service`.\nThe observability probe is the heavier I/O consumer of the two and currently competes with Gerrit\nat full priority.\n\n### R3 — the same latch on `rebar-autodeploy.service` (sibling)\n\n`infra/systemd/rebar-autodeploy.service` is also `Type=oneshot` with no `TimeoutStartSec`, driven by\n`OnUnitActiveSec=2min`, and `autodeploy.sh` contains unbounded `git clone`/`git fetch` calls. It has\nthe identical permanent-silence failure mode and it is the mechanism that keeps the box tracking\n`main`. One line, same category, fixed here.\n\n### R4 — cap journald retention in `user_data.sh`\n\n`SystemMaxUse` is set explicitly so the worst-case corpus is far below the level at which even the\nmeasured ~65 s/GB CPU/parse floor threatens the period.\n\n**This does NOT fix the running host.** `user_data.sh` executes only at instance launch. The change\nmakes future instances correct; the live box keeps its current journald configuration until an\noperator applies it. No host access is performed by this change.\n\n### R5 — sibling bounds inside the same probe\n\nThe IMDS token/region/instance-id curls and the `git ls-remote` mirror comparison run unbounded in\nthe same 5-minute probe (its sibling Gerrit REST read at the adjacent line correctly uses\n`--max-time 10`), as does the `du` over unexpected data-disk entries. Each is bounded.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 - The probe's journal read volume per run does not grow with the size of the retained\n      journal. Demonstrated by running the real script against two synthetic journals of very\n      different sizes and comparing the volume actually read, not by inspecting the source.\n- [ ] AC2 - The published delta stays CORRECT under bounding: markers arriving during an interval\n      are counted exactly once, and a following run counts only what arrived since.\n- [ ] AC3 - When the journal read fails or is cut off, the probe publishes NOTHING for that metric\n      and does not advance its cursor, honouring the `:280`/`:487` contract. No fabricated 0 and no\n      truncated delta.\n- [ ] AC4 - The rendered observability unit carries a finite start timeout strictly less than its\n      timer period, so a hung run cannot delete the timer's next elapse.\n- [ ] AC5 - `rebar-autodeploy.service` carries a finite start timeout for the same reason.\n- [ ] AC6 - Documented in the tree, at the site: bounding disk SPACE does not bound disk IOPS, epic\n      6202 does not address this failure mode, and the `user_data.sh` journald cap applies only to\n      newly launched instances.\n\n## Out of scope, deliberately\n\n- The original AC1/AC2/AC3 of this ticket (a CloudWatch alarm on root-volume IOPS, memory-pressure\n  alarming, heterogeneous admission control) are DETECTION and ADMISSION work. This change removes\n  the confirmed cause. Detection is tracked separately so that a detector is not built to replace a\n  fix; `witted-invisible-roan` already owns the memory half.\n- Applying anything to the live host. Terraform here is written and validated, never applied."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788564528603087172,"uuid":"a88de5ce-5a48-480b-8b7b-957df6d97086"}